---
/*
  The persistent chrome: the signature and MENU together, top left, and nothing
  else. Both at label scale.

  Revised 2026-08-02 (owner's call). MENU moved from the top right to sit beside
  the signature. This is not tidying, it removes a defect found in verification:
  the two marks used to sit at opposite ends of the same band of photograph, and
  a photograph can be dark at one end and bright at the other, so one polarity
  value could not keep both legible. Sharing a single local background means one
  value is always right for both (DESIGN-PLAN section 14).

  Revised 2026-08-01 (owner's call). It is fixed, so the mark stays visible over
  full-bleed media at all times, which is exactly what the primary reference
  does: Sorcinelli keeps its hairline signature in the top left over every
  full-screen image. A brand hiding its own name on every page except home is
  not restraint.

  THE CHROME STOPPED FLOATING (2026-08-11, section 87). THE OWNER'S CALL.

  It used to sit ON the photography and take a polarity measured per image
  (`overlayChrome`), swapped by an observer as frames passed beneath. Measured
  across the site, that arrangement put the brand's own name under 4.5:1 on 118
  of 152 audit faults, and 71 of 97 photograph placements had NO legible side —
  no value of that field could have fixed them, because the band held both
  extremes at once.

  Now it takes its own height in the flow, in page ground, and the photograph
  begins underneath it. The mark is ink on paper at 19.6:1, everywhere, at every
  width, on every photograph he will ever shoot, and there is nothing left to
  measure or to keep in step.

  WHAT THIS COST, said plainly: a full-bleed photograph no longer touches the
  top edge of the screen. On the shop pages the band simply continues the paper
  shipping line that was already there; on about and process it is a new strip.
  That was the trade and the owner took it, against rendered alternatives.

  THE NO-SCRIM RULE SURVIVES INTACT (standing rule 11). This is not a scrim and
  it is not a compromise version of one: nothing is laid over the photograph,
  nothing is faded, and no value between ink and paper is painted anywhere. A
  later session must not "restore" a veil under the marks believing it is
  putting something back — there was never a veil, and the alternative that used
  one was rendered, measured and rejected (section 86).
*/
import Signature from "./Signature.astro";
import Menu from "./Menu.astro";
import {localePath, type Locale} from "../lib/locales";
import {t} from "../i18n/ui";

interface Props {
  /**
   * The polarity of the PAGE. The menu panel inverts against it, so opening the
   * menu performs the site's own two-state idea (DESIGN-PLAN section 61).
   */
  pageTheme?: "light" | "dark";
  locale: Locale;
}

const {locale, pageTheme = "light"} = Astro.props;
---

{/*
  STICKY, AND NOW WITH ITS OWN FOOTPRINT (section 87). Sticky is unchanged and
  still right: it starts below the shipping line and pins to the top the moment
  that line scrolls away, so the two can never collide (section 70). What went
  is `-mb-chrome`, the negative margin that gave the band zero height in the
  flow and made it an overlay. It has its height now, and `bg-bg` paints it in
  the page's own ground — which is paper, on every page (section 82).
*/}
<header
  id="site-chrome"
  class="bg-bg text-fg sticky inset-x-0 top-0 z-30 flex h-chrome items-center gap-u3 px-margin"
>
  <a href={localePath(locale)} aria-label={t(locale, "home")} class="block">
    <Signature class="h-sig-corner" decorative />
  </a>
  <Menu locale={locale} theme={pageTheme} />

</header>
